1015:
Development is prohibited in these areas which shall be preserved as open space; nor are any development credits generated:
(1) Open water (such as ponds, lakes, and all-weather streams or rivers);
(2) Floodways (swales, shore, or bank areas regularly flooded after heavy rains);
(3) Wetlands (continuously wet or swamp areas);
(4) Areas contaminated with high levels of toxic waste.
(Ord. of 12-7-2004, art. VIII, § 8.0)

1016:
Development is prohibited in the following areas which shall be preserved as open space; development credits, restricted to 50 percent of normal zone density, may be transferred to less sensitive locations, provided the resulting lots shall conform to not less than the minimum requirements of section 50-111:
(1) 100-year floodplains and buffer areas to streams or wetlands;
(2) Slopes greater than 24 percent and ridge tops;
(3) Rock outcroppings and nonrippable bedrock within two feet of surface;
(4) Major aquifer recharge areas;
(5) Areas contaminated with moderate levels of toxic waste;
(6) Soils unsuitable for anticipated loads or sanitary percolation;
(7) Historic, scenic open space, or archaeological sites; however, development credits for such areas shall be generated at normal or full zone density.
(Ord. of 12-7-2004, art. VIII, § 8.1)

1039:
(a) Except for primary residences and accessory buildings located in agriculture, forestry, and residential zoning districts, applications for building permits shall be accompanied by two sets of complete construction plans and elevations, outline survey, topographical survey, and site plan, showing:
(1) The actual dimensions of the lot to be built upon;
(2) The size and location of each structure to be erected;
(3) The size and location of each existing structure on the lot;
(4) The size and location of any development constraints on the lot, if any;
(5) The number of dwelling units the proposed structure is designed to accommodate;
(6) The setback lines of structure on adjoining lots;
(7) The layout of off-street parking and loading spaces;
(8) The type and location of all stormwater and sanitary structures;
(9) The makeup and layout of all buffer areas required by this chapter;
(10) Such other information as may be requested for determining whether the provisions of this article are being observed;
(11) Such other information as may be requested by the zoning administrator; and
(12) Certification by the applicant that he has complied or will comply with the applicable health department regulations, the road construction ordinance, flood ordinance, soil and erosion ordinance, and all other applicable ordinances of the county.
(b) For primary residences and accessory buildings located in agriculture, forestry, and residential zoning districts, all applications for building permits shall be accompanied by two sets of complete construction plans and elevations, outline survey, and, if required, health department approval.
(Ord. of 12-7-2004, art. XII, § 12.3)

1055:
Each application to amend the text of this chapter, the official zoning maps, or the comprehensive land use map shall be filed with the zoning administrator. Applications shall be submitted in compliance with the following:
(1) Chapter text amendment applications shall include the following:
a. Name and current address of the applicant;
b. Current provisions of the text to be affected by the amendment;
c. Proposed wording of text change; and
d. Reason for the amendment request.
(2) Comprehensive land use map amendment applications shall include the following:
a. An identification of the geographic area in the county that is to have a revised land use or uses under the applicant's proposal;
b. All permitted land uses for the identified area under the existing comprehensive land use map;
c. All changes to existing land use designations that are proposed by the application;
d. All land uses immediately adjacent to the subject property under the existing comprehensive land use map plan;
e. All reasons for the amendment application;
f. Names and addresses of the owners of the land affected by the proposed amendment and their agents, if any, authorized to apply for an amendment; and
g. An initiating party shall also file any other information or supporting materials that are required by the board of commissioners, planning commission, or zoning administrator.
(3) Official zoning map amendment (rezoning) applications shall include the following:
a. A legal description of the tract to be rezoned;
b. Six copies of the tract survey, drawn to scale, showing north arrow, land lot and district, dimensions, acreage, existing structures, current public and private easements and rights-of-way, and a key map locating the tract. An architect, engineer, landscape architect or land surveyor whose state registration is current and valid shall prepare said survey. The preparer's seal and signature shall be affixed to the plat survey;
c. Six copies of the projected site plan, drawn to scale, and showing all existing and proposed:
1. Structures to be retained or to be constructed;
2. Swales, watercourses, and drainage channels;
3. Locations of off-street parking and access to and from public rights-of-way;
4. Landscaping, buffer zones, and trees greater than 30 inches in diameter two feet above the ground. Applications for rezoning to four or fewer single-family residential lots (R-1, R-2, and R-3) fronting upon existing public rights-of-way are exempted from this requirement;
d. The present and proposed zoning district or districts for the tract;
e. All existing development constraints as enumerated by article V of this chapter;
f. The names and addresses of all owners of subject tract and their agents, if any;
g. The names and addresses of all adjoining or nearby property owners within 1,000 feet. In determining the nearby property owners, the owners of road, street, or railroad rights-of-way may be disregarded; and
h. Such other pertinent additional information as may be requested by the zoning administrator, the planning commission, or the board of commissioners.
(4) An application shall be submitted at least 45 days prior to the date on which it is to be considered by the planning commission. Application fees for an application to amend this chapter, the official zoning maps, or the comprehensive land use map shall be established by the board of commissioners and made available by the zoning administrator. A fee shall not be charged for applications initiated by the zoning administrator, planning commission, or board of commissioners.
(5) An application shall not be withdrawn by the applicant after the legal advertising as required by this article shall have first appeared, except as provided in sections 50-292(a) and 50-296(a).
(6) With respect to amendments to the official zoning maps, an applicant may be required by the planning commission to file site plans, renderings, construction specifications, written development restrictions and/or other conditions as binding conditions upon the development and use of the property involved in the application. Such plans, specifications, and conditions shall be filed with the zoning administrator at least 45 days prior to the public hearing before the planning commission. If the required plans, specifications, and/or conditions have not been filed, the planning commission, at the time of the public hearing on the application, may defer any action on such application to a specific later date that will permit the planning commission to conduct another hearing to consider the applicant's proposal. At the hearing during which the deferral is granted, the planning commission may specify the later hearing date and this action shall constitute public notice of such hearing and no additional notices shall be required prior to the hearing so scheduled. The date designated for action on the application shall allow the applicant reasonable time to comply with the filing requirements of section.
(Ord. of 12-7-2004, art. XIV, § 14.2)

1059:
The following standards governing the exercise of the zoning power are adopted in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 36-66-5(b):
(1) The existing land uses and zoning classification of nearby property;
(2) The suitability of the subject property for the zoned purposes;
(3) The extent to which the property values of the subject property are diminished by the particular zoning restrictions;
(4) The extent to which the diminution of property values of the subject property promotes the health, safety, morals, or general welfare of the public;
(5) The relative gain to the public, as compared to the hardship imposed upon the individual property owner;
(6) Whether the subject property has a reasonable economic use as currently zoned;
(7) The length of time the property has been vacant as zoned considered in the context of land development in the area in the vicinity of the property;
(8) Whether the proposed zoning will be a use that is suitable in view of the use and development of adjacent and nearby property;
(9) Whether the proposed zoning will adversely affect the existing use or usability of adjacent or nearby property;
(10) Whether the zoning proposal is in conformity with the policies and intent of the land use plan;
(11) Whether the zoning proposal will result in a use which will or could cause an excessive or burdensome use of existing streets, transportation facilities, utilities, or schools; and
(12) Whether there are other existing or changing conditions affecting the use and development of the property, which give supporting grounds for either approval or disapproval of the zoning proposal.
(Ord. of 12-7-2004, art. XIV, § 14.6)

1062:
Any use may be authorized by a conditional use permit may be approved by the county board of commissioners only if:
(1) A proper application has been filed in accordance with the requirements of this chapter not otherwise permitted within a given zoning district;
(2) A favorable recommendation has been received from the planning commission in accordance with the provisions of article X of this chapter;
(3) The applicant is in compliance with the particular conditions for the proposed conditional use that is required by this chapter;
(4) The applicant is in compliance with conditions imposed by the board of commissioners for the purpose of reducing the harmful effects of the use on surrounding uses and assuring compatibility with surrounding uses; and
(5) The board of commissioners determines that the benefits of and need for the proposed conditional use are greater than any possible depreciating effects and damages to the neighboring properties.
(Ord. of 12-7-2004, art. XV, § 15.1)
